  "proof_route": [
    "isClosed_critical: fderiv of a C1 function is continuous, so the critical set is closed and needs no measurability hypothesis",
    "exists_lipschitzOnWith_critical_cover: exists_partition_approximatesLinearOn_of_hasFDerivWithinAt gives pairwise disjoint measurable pieces with approximating linear maps that are VALUES OF THE DERIVATIVE; on the critical set those values are zero, so ApproximatesLinearOn with linear part 0 is literally an epsilon-Lipschitz estimate. The empty critical set is a separate branch since the value clause needs nonemptiness",
    "ball localization: for radius R and epsilon = (k+1)^-1, apply L1 on each piece D n = C inter closedBall inter T n, sum the majorants with lintegral_tsum and ENNReal.tsum_mul_left, and use measure_iUnion on the disjoint measurable pieces to get eilenbergConstant V * volume (closedBall 0 R) * (k+1)^-1",
    "the pointwise infimum over k is measurable (Measurable.iInf), still dominates, and its integral is below a sequence tending to 0 (ge_of_tendsto'), so it is zero and vanishes a.e. by lintegral_eq_zero_iff; finiteness of volume (closedBall 0 R) is what makes this work, hence the localization",
    "removing the ball: ae_all_iff over the radii plus Metric.iUnion_closedBall_nat and measure_iUnion_null"
  ],
  "not_proved": [
    "Sard's theorem: sharp C^(finrank) Analysis.sard and Analysis.ae_forall_fderiv_ne_zero, both still carrying sorryAx; L10 does not imply it",
    "L8 weighted graph area on an open parameter domain",
    "L9 regular-part C1 coarea",
    "L11 general C1 weighted coarea and L12 the Lipschitz version",
    "the weighted coarea EQUALITY Analysis.lintegral_enorm_gradient_mul_eq_lintegral_levelSet and its C1 specialization Isoperimetry.lintegral_enorm_gradient_mul_eq_lintegral_levelSet, both still direct holes",
    "L6/L6a/L7 Whitney C1 extension and exact C1 pieces",
    "sharpness of the Eilenberg constant",
    "smoothConstant_le_minkowskiConstant",
    "smoothConstant_mul_lintegral_min_superlevel_le",
    "equality of the two Cheeger constants"
  ],
